# Onganda repository rules

Before changing UI, read `skills/onganda-design-system/SKILL.md`, `DESIGN_GUIDELINES.md`, and the relevant public export in `src/design-system/`.

Read `DESIGN_SYSTEM.md` §4 (Color Roles) and §5 (Controls) before styling anything. They are the enforceable rules and every value in them is measured.

Before changing design data, read:

- `onganda-colors.ts` for the palette and surface tokens.
- `onganda-roles.ts` for `roles()`, `ALPHA`, `statusFor()`, `onColor()`, `SYSTEM_COLOR`, `COLOR_POLICY`.
- `onganda-types.ts` for shared types.
- `onganda-content.ts` for system and editorial data.

Style from roles, not from the palette. Call `roles(isDark)` and read `surface`, `text`, `line`, `actionBg`, `accentInk`, `fieldBorder`. Being in `COLOR` does not make a token available for any purpose.

Never write a raw hex, an `rgba()`, or a generic Tailwind colour class in a component. All three fail `npm run check:design`.

The primary action is ochre with a char black label and a bark rust edge; the in-card accent is mahangu gold with a mopane edge; danger is bark rust with a fired red edge. Char black is ink and the inverse band, never the primary fill. Ochre is a fill, with char black on it. As ink on a light surface it is 2.4:1, so the light-mode accent ink is bark rust — use `accentInk`, never a raw token. Fired red is an indicator only: status marks and invalid borders, never behind small text and never error-message text. Mahangu gold, mopane brown, and dye plum identify systems and carry chart series; they reach chrome only through roles, never picked by a component. The palette has no green and no blue; do not add one. Read status through `statusFor(name, isDark)`.

Colour is never the only carrier of state: an invalid field has a border, a marker, and a message; a status mark has a label and a hairline ring.

Never hand-roll a control. Use `PixelButton`, `PixelInput`, `PixelTextarea`, `PixelSelect`, `PixelField`, `PixelCheckbox`, `PixelRadio`, `PixelToggle`. One primary action per view. No rounded corners anywhere. A control on an inverse surface (footer, ticker, black lab card) must be passed `isDark`.

A control that is not in the `/showcase` Control Standards section does not exist yet. That section has its own light/dark switch — verify both modes.

System colours mean membership. Mahangu gold, mopane brown, and dye plum identify Ombiga, Etiti, and Oshitoo; bark rust identifies Oshini. They reach chrome only through roles; a component never binds one directly.

Composition matters as much as colour. An editorial surface (a public page, the showcase) carries the `SectionHeader` label/headline rhythm, `WeaveOverlay` on its grounds, pixel marks, pixel iconography, an inverse band and real `MOTION`; `tests/browser/composition.spec.ts` checks it. A product surface (a tool, a dashboard) is quiet chrome with the colour on the work: an ochre primary, each section its own accent, each peer item `accentFor(i)` on its spine and a filled ordinal tag, state through `statusFor`. Cards are flat — no texture on cards, and never texture as a selected or active state. No dotted or dashed lines.

Open `/showcase` before building. It is the inventory, not a demo: Control Standards for every control, Product Patterns for dialog/table/notice/empty/loading/tabs/progress, plus diagrams, heatmap, roadmap, charts, icons, maps and publications. Use `PixelDialog`, `PixelTable`, `PixelNotice`, `PixelEmptyState`, `PixelSkeleton`, `PixelTabs`, `PixelProgress` rather than inventing equivalents. Give every surface that loads an empty, loading and error state.

Keep the pixel and type split. Use pixel geometry for brand marks, icons, diagrams, and textures. Use clean Inter or Arial for reading.

Reuse existing tokens, primitives, icons, and motion patterns. Spread `MOTION` presets rather than retyping transition values.

Keep Oshiwambo system names first. Treat `/showcase` as the reference implementation. Preserve the intentional roles of `/`, `/full`, and `/showcase`.

Run `npm run check` and `npm run test:browser` before completing a UI change.
